{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-haiku-5-5","id":"msg_011CgJYvDjxhXSJoXgZvgbrm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Answer: the fix is in openmrs-core's env-var key normalization, and it is the `NON_NORMALIZED_KEYS` whitelist.** Falkon has no record of TRUNK-6646 (no hits for the ticket key), and I had no commit-history tool, so I could not confirm the commit SHA or PR number. The mechanism below is from the current code and the test's doc comment, not from the diff itself.\n\n**How the fix works**\n\n- `openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java:197-201` whitelists `CREATE_DATABASE_USERNAME`, `CREATE_DATABASE_PASSWORD`, `CREATE_USER_USERNAME`, `CREATE_USER_PASSWORD` and other keys. These keep their underscores when normalized.\n- `openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java:1948-1953` (`normalizeEnvVariableKey`) lowercases whitelisted keys and leaves underscores alone. Every other key has `_` turned into `.`. Before the whitelist, `CREATE_DATABASE_USERNAME` would become `create.database.username`, which never matches the `create_database_username` property that the loader reads.\n- `openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java:340-343` applies the normalized env vars to the script properties, and `:370-373` reads `create_database_username` and `create_database_password` from them.\n- `openmrs-core/web/src/test/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilterNormalizationTest.java:1-92` tests this directly. Its doc comment says it covers \"the fix for env-var key normalization that was dropping create_database_* and create_user_* credentials in scripted setup.\"\n\n**The fallback to root**\n\n- `openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java:533-550` is the auto-install path. It defaults the create-database username to `connection.username` or `postgres`, then sets the password from `databaseRootPassword`. If the env values are dropped, this is where the wrong credentials come from.\n- `openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationWizardModel.java:130,140` holds the `root` and empty-password defaults that the fallback relies on.\n\n**Not verified**\n\n- The pre-fix behavior is inferred from the code and test. I did not read the diff.\n- `startup-init.sh:145` maps `OMRS_DB_USERNAME` to `connection.username` for the Docker path. It is related but I did not trace it further.\n\n```json\n[\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java\", \"startLine\": 197, \"endLine\": 201},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java\", \"startLine\": 1948, \"endLine\": 1953},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java\", \"startLine\": 334, \"endLine\": 373},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilter.java\", \"startLine\": 533, \"endLine\": 550},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/web/src/test/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationFilterNormalizationTest.java\", \"startLine\": 1, \"endLine\": 92},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/web/src/main/java/org/openmrs/web/filter/initialization/InitializationWizardModel.java\", \"startLine\": 130, \"endLine\": 140},\n  {\"path\": \"openmrs-core/startup-init.sh\", \"startLine\": 145, \"endLine\": 145}\n]\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2028,"cache_read_input_tokens":22489,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2028},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"sta